VIDEO VISIT  I performed this consultation using real-time Telehealth tools, including a live video connection between my location and the patient's location. Prior to initiating the consultation, I obtained informed verbal consent to perform this consultation using Telehealth tools and answered all the questions about the Telehealth interaction.      Mr. ***** is a 61 y.o. male whom I am seeing as a video visit at the <1>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</1> for follow-up of his metastatic <2>pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma</2>.    History of Present Illness, reviewed:  This pleasant gentleman initially presented to medical attention in December 2019 with <3>urinary urge incontinence</3> (<4>urinalysis</4> negative), with associated <5>suprapubic discomfort</5>. He also endorsed <6>weight loss</6> of 10 pounds since June, ***** with the beginning of the *****-19 pandemic, but otherwise no <7>fever</7> or <8>infectious symptoms</8>. Ultimately, as his symptoms progressed, he underwent a <9>CT scan of the abdomen/pelvis</9> (01/24/20) that demonstrated a <10>3.5 cm pancreatic tail mass</10> with <11>occlusion of the splenic vein</11> and <12>encasement of the splenic artery</12> with resultant <13>upper abdominal collaterals</13>. Possible <14>abutment of the mass on the stomach</14> and <15>left adrenal gland</15> was seen. Additionally, there was <16>multifocal hepatic metastatic disease</16> with resultant <17>mild right intrahepatic biliary dilatation</17>, as well as <18>diffuse circumferential rectosigmoid wall thickening</18>, possibly representing <19>proctitis</19> for which he received a 2 week course of <20>Septra</20> -> <21>ciprofloxacin</21> which provided mild pain relief. <22>Laboratory studies</22> (02/09/2020) showed his <23>liver enzymes</23> to be moderately elevated, with <24>ALT</24> 114 and <25>AST</25> 71, and his <26>CA 19-9</26> to be markedly abnormal at <27>19,362 U/mL</27>.    He eventually underwent a <28>CT guided liver biopsy</28> on 02/29/20, and <29>pathology</29> was consistent with <30>adenocarcinoma of pancreatobiliary origin</30>. He also subsequently was found to have <31>cytologically-confirmed peritoneal carcinomatosis</31>.
``` 
