 by ascites for which he received <1>furosemide</1> and <2>spironolactone</2>. He was on the <3>furosemide</3> and <4>spironolactone</4> for 3-4 weeks. lost 30 lbs perioperatively.     08/30/13: <5>CT chest wo contrast</5> showed stable appearance of the chest without <6>pulmonary nodules</6> or <7>lymphadenopathy</7> and an ovoid soft tissue density is seen just inferior to the <8>pancreas</8> on the last ***** of the <9>chest CT</9>.    11/23/2012: <10>Restaging MRI AP</10> showed multiple enhancing lesions in the <11>liver</11> with the largest in the right lobe liver measuring up to 10 mm on the <12>arterial phase</12>. The <13>arterial phase imaging</13> was somewhat limited due to <14>portal venous contamination</14>. An attempted <15>ultrasound guided biopsy</15> of the <16>liver lesion</16> seen on the 11/23/2012 <17>MRI</17> but the <18>ultrasound</18> failed to reveal any <19>nodules</19> in the <20>liver</20> that warranted <21>biopsy</21>. A followup <22>triphasic liver CT</22> also did not visualized on the <23>hypervascular lesion</23> seen on the November 24 <24>MRI</24>.     03/04/2013: <25>Restaging MRI AP</25> showed multiple small foci of persistent <26>arterial phase enhancement</26> within the <27>liver</27>, the largest of which measures 1.5 cm that is better seen on the current <28>MRI</28>. There is a 10 mm <29>nodule</29> at the posterior right hepatic lobe is not well appreciated on current exam. The remainder of the <30>nodules</30> appear stable.     08/30/2013: <31>Restaging MRI AP</31> showed decrease in number of enhancing foci in the <32>liver</32>. <33>Restaging chest CT</33> showed no ***** in the <34>chest</34>.    03/10/2014: <35>Restaging MRI AP</35> showed multiple small arterially enhancing <36>liver lesions</36> are again seen, the largest measuring 1.6 cm in the lateral aspect of the right hepatic lobe. A few of these <37>lesions</37> are seen to enhance more prominently on the current study compared to the prior study of 08/30/2013. However, the <38>lesions</38> were even more intensely enhancing and apparently larger on the more remote study of 03/04/2013. It remains unclear if these variations in <39>lesion</39> conspicuity and size are related to fluctuations of true pathology versus differential 
