ner_results: ```plaintext
 to intermediate sized cells with minimal pleomorphism, ample cytoplasm, round to ovoid nuclei, and speckled chromatin. Mitotic count is less than one per 10 *****. There is no necrosis. <1>Immunohistochemical stains</1> performed at the original institution and submitted for our review are interpreted as follows in the tumor cells: - <2>Synaptophysin</2>: Positive. - *****-67 Cannot evaluate (H&E section not submitted for comparison, by report 3%). H&E sections from the <3>retroperitoneal lymph node</3> (*****-*****) shows an infiltrating tumor in a background of fibrovascular tissue. The tumor is composed of trabeculae of intermediate sized cells with occasional ***** formation. The tumor cells show mild atypia, ample cytoplasm, round to ovoid nuclei, and conspicuous nucleoli. Mitotic count is at most 3 per 10 high power fields. There is no necrosis. No prominent nucleoli are seen to suggest a large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma. The amount of cytoplasm argues against a small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma <4>Immunohistochemical stains</4> performed at the original institution and reviewed at ***** show the following results in tumor cells:   - <5>Synaptophysin</5>: Positive.  - *****-67: <6>19.7 % proliferation index</6> (on 2000 cell count).     Overall, the clinical, morphologic and immunohistochemical findings indicate <7>neuroendocrine tumor of pancreatic origin</7> with a possible metastasis to a <8>lymph node</8> (although admittedly no normal nodal tissue is identified in the retroperitoneal biopsy). Based on a pancreatic primary, with 3 mitoses / 10 hpf, a proliferation index = 19.7%, this would be classified as a <9>WHO grade 2 neuroendocrine tumor</9>.       ASSESSMENT & PLAN    Patient Active Problem List    Diagnosis Date Noted    <10>Primary pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor</10> 09/28/2012     Mr. ***** ***** ***** is a 52 y.o. male who presents to our <11>GI Oncology practice</11> at the <12>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</12> for follow up of his <13>metastatic pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor</13>
