VIDEO VISIT  I performed this consultation using real-time Telehealth tools, including a live video connection between my location and the patient's location. Prior to initiating the consultation, I obtained informed verbal consent to perform this consultation using Telehealth tools and answered all the questions about the Telehealth interaction.    Ms. ***** is a 40 y.o. female whom I am seeing as a video visit at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of her  <1>pancreatic cancer</1>.    Oncologic History:    June 2020: Pt presented to PCP for 5mo sharp <2>epigastric pain</2>, associated with hot foods and when hungry.    06/29/20: <3>US abdomen</3>: No <4>cholelithiasis</4> or acute <5>cholecystitis</5>. No biliary ductal dilation. Echogenic liver, consistent with <6>fatty infiltration</6> in the absence of clinical evidence of other diffuse or chronic hepatic processes.  2 <7>cystic structures</7> seen in the left upper quadrant, largest measuring 6.9 cm. Although simple appearing, the origin of these <8>cysts</8> is difficult to determine sonographically  07/18/20: ***** *****: ***** and <9>cystic mass</9> of the <10>tail of the pancreas</10> (the smaller measuring 2.0 x 2.9 cm and the larger 6.1 x 4.9 cm. A <11>solid component</11> is appreciated which measures approximately 5.1 x 4.1 cm), showing extension beyond the pancreas to the upper left <12>retroperitoneum</12>, abutting the <13>aorta</13> and <14>celiac/superior mesenteric arteries</14>. The mass may cause <15>thrombosis</15> of the <16>left renal vein</16> as there are left renal venous collaterals, enlargement of the left kidney, and adjacent left <17>perinephric fluid</17>. Obstruction of the <18>splenic vein</18> is also suspected. Locally aggressive <19>tumor of the pancreas</19> is suspected. The solid and cystic appearance is atypical for <20>pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma</20>. Given the patient's age and appearance, <21>solid pseudopapillary epithelial tumor of the pancreas</21> can be also considered. <22>Biopsy</22> is recommended. ***** <23>pancreatic mass</23>. This finding will be reviewed by hepatobiliary surgery,
``` 
