Mr. ***** is a 80 y.o. male who comes to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center in consultation for his <1>pancreatic head adenocarcinoma</1>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This pleasant Chinese-speaking gentleman with multiple medical co-morbidities originally presented in June 2018 with subacute onset of <2>epigastric/RUQ pain</2> and fairly subtle <3>jaundice symptoms</3>. Initial diagnostic w/u at the time included an <4>abdominal U/S</4> (07/23/2018) notable for marked <5>intra and extrahepatic biliary duct dilation</5>, with no obstructing mass identified but a possible <6>choledochal cyst</6>. Subsequent <7>CT scans</7> that same day confirmed <8>biliary dilatation</8>, felt likely 2o to <9>obstructive choledocholithiasis</9>, with associated <10>cholecystitis</10> but again no discrete mass appreciated. <11>MRCP</11> (07/25/2018) failed to visualize a <12>pancreatic head or common bile duct mass</12> at the location of distal common bile duct caliber change; with irregular thickening and enhancement of the <13>gallbladder fundus</13>. <14>ERCP</14> was performed on 07/24/2018, with extraction a <15>CBD stone</15> and placement of two <16>7F x 10 cm stents</16> bilaterally above a high grade stricture in the intrapancreatic portion of the common bile duct. He has since undergone several additional <17>ERCPs</17>, most recently on 09/30/2018, for <18>biliary stent exchanges</18>. Additionally, <19>EUS</19> on 10/08/2018 demonstrated a <20>20 X 16mm irregular and hypoechoic mass</20> in the head of the pancreas, invading into the common bile duct; as well as a hypodensity of the ***** that was suspicious for <21>tumor involvement of the gallbladder</21>. <22>FN biopsy</22> (<23>25G SharkCore needle</23>) confirmed <24>adenocarcinoma</24>. The patient's most recent imaging studies consisted of repeat <25>CT scan</25> performed on 09/17/2018, notable for interval development of <26>4.8 cm mass</26> centered in the gallbladder fundus with <27>hepatic invasion</27> concerning for <28>gallbladder carcinoma</28>; as well
``` 
