ner_results: ```plaintext
Mr. ***** is a 37 y.o. male who comes to our <1>GI Oncology practice</1> at the <2>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</2> in consultation for his <3>metastatic pancreatic cancer</3>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This gentleman first developed <4>nausea</4>/<5>vomiting</5>, waxing/waning, beginning in <6>November 2014</6>, but progressively worsening over time. <7>Labs</7> drawn by his <8>PCP</8> demonstrated an elevated <9>lipase</9>, prompting his presentation to his local <10>ED</10> on <11>01/06/2015</11> for further <12>w/u</12> of presumed <13>pancreatitis</13>. <14>Abdominal U/S</14> showed multiple <15>liver masses</15> consistent with <16>malignancy</16>. Subsequent <17>CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast</17> showed a <18>6 cm irregular mass</18> in the <19>body of the pancreas</19> with encasement of the <20>hepatic and splenic arteries</20> and invasion of the <21>third portion of the duodenum</21> with obstruction, and multiple <22>hepatic metastases</22>. <23>CT-PET</23> confirmed a <24>pancreatic body primary tumor</24> infiltrating into the <25>left adrenal fossa</25> and to the <26>third portion of the duodenum</26>: <27>6 x 4.6 x 5.2 cm</27>/<28>SUV 5.1</28>; extensive, confluent <29>liver metastases</29>; numerous <30>bilateral pulmonary nodules</30>; at least <31>3 hypermetabolic skeletal metastases</31> (<32>sacrum</32>, <33>mid T5 vertebral body</33>); small volume <34>ascites</34>; <35>omental nodularity</35> and <36>peritoneal thickening</36>; and numerous <37>mesenteric lymph nodes</37>. <38>Spine MRI</38> showed focal signal abnormality in the <39>S1 vertebral body</39>, and areas of <40>disc protrusion</40> at <41>T12-L1</41> and <42>L3-4</42>. During the patient's hospital admission, he was maintained on a <43>liquid diet</43> and <44>rx'ed with anti-emetics</44>. <45>Liver biopsy</45> was performed on <46>01/08/2015</46>, which apparently showed <47>metastatic moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma of pancreatic or biliary origin</47> (formal report N/A). The patient's present review of systems was reviewed and notable for the following: - <48>PO intake</48> modest, including soups and some solids. No/minimal postprandial <49>vomiting</49>; <50>nausea</50> under reasonable control on <51>anti-emetics</51> -
``` 
