ner_results: ```plaintext
 Pancreatic duct <1>dilatation</1> in the residual <2>body</2> and <3>tail</3>.  <4>Soft tissue density</4> demonstrated posterior to the proximal <5>SMA</5> with partial encasement measuring <6>1.5 cm</6>. Interval appearance of an ill-defined <7>sclerotic density</7> within the <8>L2 vertebral body</8> when compared to <9>08/31/2011 scans</9>.      ASSESSMENT & PLAN  In summary, ***** ***** ***** is a <10>69 y.o. female</10> who presents to our <11>GI Oncology practice</11> at the <12>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</12> for evaluation of her <13>recurrent pancreatic adenocarcinoma</13>. Following her original ***** <14>operation</14>, she received adjuvant <15>gemcitabine</15>, mostly dosed in a somewhat unusual fashion of once-monthly. She now has evidence of <16>recurrent disease</16> based on suspicious <17>radiographic findings</17> (<18>local recurrence</18> plus presumed <19>metastatic disease</19> to the <20>lymph nodes</20> and <21>L2 vertebral body</21>) and a rising *****-<22>9 biomarker</22>. Apparently the patient had a repeat <23>PET/CT</23> obtained last week, while off <24>treatment</24> for the past month-plus, although we currently do not have the report or the images available for review. We will work on obtaining these as well as getting a full set of <25>laboratory studies</25> during patient's visit today.     Her ***** off-treatment is reasonably good, and depending on the extent and progression of her disease we will decide on the need and timing of re-initiating <26>chemotherapy</26>. The patient understands that such <27>treatment</27> would be for prolongation of life (but not cure) and possibly <28>symptom palliation</28>. As the patient has concerns about <29>chemotherapy</29> and the side effects related to <30>systemic therapy</30>, our recommendation would be try <31>treatment</31> with ***** or ***** and see how well she tolerates it. We reviewed some of the potential <32>side effects</32> including but not limited to : <33>bone marrow suppression</33> increasing the risk of <34>infections</34>, <35>bleeding</35> and <36>transfusions</36>, <37>nausea</37>, <38>vomiting</38>, <39>diarrhea</39>, <40>cold sensitivity</40>, <41>mucositis</41>, <42>neuropathy</42>, and <43>hand-foot syndrome</43>. We provided the patient and his daughter to ask 
