ner_results: ```plaintext
Mr. ***** is a 79 y.o. male who returns to our <1>GI Oncology practice</1> at the <2>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</2> for follow-up of his localized <3>pancreatic cancer</3>, originally diagnosed in May 2013 after presenting with <4>obstructive jaundice</4>. Despite potentially resectable disease, the decision has been made to defer on <5>surgery</5> due to his advanced age and high operative risk. He started on <6>chemotherapy</6> with alternating-week schedule of <7>gemcitabine</7> (800 mg/m2) plus <8>capecitabine</8> (650 mg/m2 BID), beginning on 08/25/2013 (28 day cycles). Treatment was initially complicated by several hospitalizations due to <9>ascending cholangitis</9>/<10>bacteremia</10>; he does have a <11>metallic stent</11> as well as <12>bilateral plastic stents</12> in place which are periodically exchanged. His <13>capecitabine</13> was ultimately held beginning midway through cycle 4a of treatment, and he continued with single-agent <14>gemcitabine</14> administered on a ***** dosing schedule (days 1 and 15 q28 days) through cycle #6 in February 2014. After that point, I referred him to our <15>Rad Onc faculty practice</15> for consideration of consolidation (<16>chemo</16>)<17>RT</17>, including possibly *****, but the decision was made to defer on this given the absence of radiographically measurable disease. Also of note, the patient was admitted to ***** campus from 6.4 through 04/30/2014 due to progressive <18>hypoxia</18> at home, and the more acute development of <19>fevers</19>/<20>rigors</20> and <21>altered mental status</21>. Inpatient evaluation included fairly unremarkable <22>CT scans</22> (see below), <23>echocardiogram</23> showing normal <24>LV function</24>, and <25>Pulmonary evaluation</25> with identification of <26>restrictive pulmonary physiology</26> (? <27>kyphoscoliosis</27> plus <28>generalized weakness</28>, other etiology?), improved with use of <29>BiPAP</29>. He was also hospitalized at end of May for <30>mental status changes</30> a/w poorly controlled <31>HTN</31>; <32>brain MRI</32> suggests some <33>amyloid angiopathy</33>. The patient has now been off all <34>therapy</34> for the past 5 months.
``` 
