ner_results: ```plaintext
Subjective:    This is a very pleasant 50-year-old woman with the aforementioned <1>diagnosis</1>.  *****, her mother was under our care for <2>metastatic pancreatic adenocarcinoma</2>. Her mother had been doing well but suddenly developed <3>sepsis</3> and a <4>bowel perforation</4> and died just prior to her daughter's <5>diagnosis</5>.    The patient's <6>diagnosis</6> was triggered by the <7>symptoms</7> of <8>dyspepsia</8> that were triggered by food.  This started about a month ago.  She's also had a few months of unexplained <9>weight loss</9>, which she thought might be due to stress.  She had also noticed <10>early satiety</10> and had <11>back pain</11> that radiated to her <12>abdomen</12>.  She was also <13>fatigued</13>.    <14>CT scan</14> dated March 03 showed a <15>mass</15> involving the <16>pancreatic head</16> encasing the <17>proximal celiac axis</17> and region of the <18>portal mesenteric confluent</18>.  This was confirmed on <19>EUS</19> on March 16 at which time a <20>fine needle aspirate</20> was done confirming <21>adenocarcinoma</21>.    When we initially saw her, she appeared to have impending <22>biliary duct obstruction</22>.  We referred her for an <23>ERCP</23> and she was also found to have some threat of <24>obstruction</24> in the <25>duodenum</25>. We also repeated her <26>CT scan</26> and this again confirmed <27>locally advanced disease</27>.    We then started treatment with modified *****. She completed <28>12 cycles</28>. She initially did not not tolerate it well. However, we reduced her doses across the board and this helped a lot. She then went on <29>surveillance</29>. During this time, she required placement of a <30>duodenal stent</30>.    We saw her in December, she clearly had progressing <31>disease</31> with new <32>ascites</32>. She also had <33>tumor ingrowth</33> in her <34>duodenal stent</34> requiring placement of a <35>new stent</35> within the stent.    We then started <36>gemcitabine</36> and <37>Abraxane</37>. She's had one full <38>cycle</38>. She is tolerating <39>therapy</39> reasonably well. Her <40>appetite</40> is variable. She's lost <41>14 pounds</41>. She is quite <42>constipated</42> from her <43>narcotics</43> but can get relief with <44>senna</44> and <45>docusate sodium</45>.    Her <46>pain</46> is not optimally controlled.
``` 
