ner_results: Mr. ***** is a 70 y.o. male who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of his <1>metastatic pancreatic cancer</1>, originally diagnosed in March 2013 after presenting with <2>progressive abdominal discomfort</2>. <3>CT scan</3> at that time demonstrated a <4>3.9 cm pancreatic tail necrotic mass</4>, extending into the <5>splenic hilum</5> and abutting the <6>stomach</6>, with surrounding stranding in the fat which extends toward the <7>L kidney</7> and <8>splenic flexure of the colon</8>; with encasement of the <9>splenic artery</9>. Also noted were adjacent <10>LNs 9 mm in size</10>; <11>cystic liver lesions up to 3.6 cm in size</11>; <12>small free fluid in the pelvis</12> with <13>pelvic/peritoneal nodules up to 1.4 cm in size</13>; <14>nodular thickening along greater curvature of the stomach</14>; and <15>L ureteral obstruction 2o to a 2.3 cm retroperitoneal nodule causing moderate hydronephrosis</15>. <16>Endoscopic FNA of the pancreatic mass</16> on 03/27/2013 confirmed <17>adenocarcinoma</17>. The patient underwent <18>ureteral stent placement</18> on 03/28/2013. Initial treatment consisted of *****, to which he responded well with interval imaging demonstrating reduction in both his <19>primary tumor</19> and measurable distant sites of disease. However, he did develop some cumulative <20>cytopenias</20> and <21>peripheral sensory neuropathy</21> ultimately requiring holding of the <22>oxaliplatin</22> beginning with <23>cycle #6</23>. He then continued with ***** alone, with an eventual spacing of his dosing schedule to <24>q3 weeks</24>. Between the ***** and the *****, he completed <25>18 cycles</25> in total through February 2014. Following his initial consultation with me in February 2014, I suggested he meet Dr. ***** of our Surgical faculty practice to discuss his <26>surgical options</26>, given the absence of measurable metastatic disease on his most recent <27>CT scans</27> and excellent durable disease control. Repeat imaging here at ***** on 05/22/2014 confirmed only the presence of an <28>ill-defined hypodense pancreatic tail mass measuring approximately 2.0</28> 
