This is an independent visit    PATIENT IDENTIFICATION: Ms. ***** is a 61 y.o. female who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of her diagnosis of <1>pancreas adenocarcinoma metastatic to liver</1> currently participating in *****#***** <2>Nivolumab</2>/<3>Abraxane</3>/<4>Gemcitabine</4> ***** clinical trial.     ONCOLOGIC HISTORY:   She initially presented in November 2017 with what was thought to be <5>gallstone pancreatitis</5>. She underwent a <6>cholecystectomy</6>. Following this, she had recurrent <7>pancreatitis</7> and <8>obstructive jaundice</8> requiring a <9>stent</9>. Despite repeated imaging with <10>ultrasound</10>, no pancreatic abnormalities were identified.     On 02/25/2018 ***** ***** revealed a <11>mass in the head of the pancreas</11>. She underwent a <12>core and fine-needle aspirate</12> of the mass and this showed <13>adenocarcinoma</13>. She was apparently thought to be a candidate for <14>resection</14>. Prior to resection, <15>CT</15>/<16>PET</16> was done on 02/28/18. The CT suggested a possible <17>cyst in the left lobe of the liver</17> and a follow up PET suggested this was <18>PET avid</18>. She was told she had <19>metastatic disease</19> and has been scheduled for treatment with <20>gemcitabine</20> and <21>Abraxane</21>.    03/21/18: Patient had initial consultation with Dr. *****. We were hopeful that the PET was a false positive. An <22>MRI</22> to further evaluate the liver was recommended.     03/24/18: MRI showed <23>multiple liver lesions</23>, with rapid progression compared with 02/28/2018.    03/29/18: Signed informed consent for the ***** trial *****#*****
``` 
