SUBJECTIVE:  ***** ***** is a 67 year old gentleman with a history of <1>*****'s syndrome</1> and <2>hyperlipidemia</2> who initially presented to his primary care doctor (***** *****) with 3 months of <3>loose stools</3>, 2 weeks of <4>abdominal discomfort</4>, and <5>unintentional weight loss</5> (baseline weight 175 lbs --> 158 lbs with intermittent fasting --> ***** at 145 lb). He underwent a <6>CT A/P</6> on 01/30/22 that demonstrated <7>2.7 cm ill-defined pancreatic head mass</7> in addition to a <8>small bowel obstruction</8> (which was later felt to be an "overread").     He was admitted to ***** ***** Medical Center and underwent <9>EUS/ERCP with sphincterotomy</9> (***** ***** - ***** *****) on 01/31/22 that demonstrated a <10>25 mm pancreatic head mass</10> with <11>upstream biliary and pancreatic ductal dilatation</11> requiring <12>plastic biliary stent placement</12>. <13>FNA of the pancreatic head mass</13> showed <14>adenocarcinoma of ductal origin</14>.       <15>CT Chest</15> on 01/31/22 to complete staging did not show evidence of <16>distant intra-thoracic metastatic disease</16>. <17>CA 19-9</17> was <18>228</18> on 01/31/22. He saw Dr ***** ***** for <19>surgical consultation</19> on 02/18/22 and was recommended <20>pancreatic protocol CT</20> and <21>neoadjuvant treatment</21>.      He met with his local oncologist Dr ***** on 02/20/22 and had discussed <22>neoadjuvant treatment</22> with *****. Subsequently, he had a consultation with us and elected to have his treatment at *****.    He has now had <23>8 cycles</23>. In <24>cycle 1</24>, he had <25>fever</25> but a <26>negative infectious workup</26>. In <27>cycle 4</27>, he had exacerbation of underlying <28>gout</28>.  During <29>cycle 5</29>, he developed <30>biliary ductal obstruction</30> with <31>cholangitis</31> and <32>sepsis</32> and was hospitalized. He later experienced <33>severe neutropenia</33> and was started on <34>growth factor</34>.    He feels pretty ***** out from his <35>chemotherapy</35>. He denies any <36>cancer related pain</36> but continues to have flares of <37>gouty arthritis</37>. He manages his <38>nausea</38> with frequent <39>Compazine</39>. He is experiencing <40>mild neuropathy</40> and <41>cold sensitivity</41> primarily on his hands. This usually  resolves with in a couple of days after treatment. He has <42>progressive fatigue</42> and naps daily. He is losing some <43>hair</43> on the top of his head. He tends to be <44>anxious</44> at the time of his <45>CT scan</45>.
