ner_results: Mr. ***** is a 63 y.o. male who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of his <1>metastatic pancreatic cancer</1>, <2>BRCA2-associated</2>, currently receiving <3>salvage chemotherapy</3> with *****. He had previously been enrolled in our <4>phase I clinical trial</4> evaluating the combination of ***** plus the <5>oral hedgehog inhibitor IPI-926</5>; we ultimately had to take him off treatment after <6>6 cycles</6> (and <7>two dose reductions</7> in the *****), despite evidence of good disease control, due to significant cumulative <8>fatigue</8>. He subsequently resumed therapy consisting of <9>reduced-dose biweekly *****</9> in <10>December 2011</10>, and completed <11>12 cycles</11> in total with excellent disease control. Following a <12>chemotherapy holiday</12> since <13>June 2012</13>, he demonstrated radiographic evidence of further <14>disease progression</14> (<15>locoregional recurrence</15> in the <16>pancreatic tail</16>, plus <17>nodal and mesenteric implants</17>), on which basis we resumed him on ***** in <18>November</18>. He received <19>6 cycles</19>, well-tolerated overall; however, <20>CT scan</20> on <21>02/20/2013</21> demonstrated modest disease progression, including a new <22>right hepatic 1.1 cm lesion</22> and increased <23>soft tissue density around the celiac artery</23> and enlargement of <24>retroperitoneal lymph nodes</24>. On this basis, we elected to switch him to the combination of <25>gemcitabine</25> plus <26>*****-paclitaxel</26> on an <27>every-other-week dosing schedule</27> (days 1 and 15 in <28>28-day cycles</28>). He has now completed <29>3 full cycles</29> of treatment. On present review of systems, the patient notes that he has been tolerating current treatment very well, with <30>mild fatigue</30>, <31>flu-like symptoms</31> and <32>skeletal pains</32> that last <33>3-4 days</33> following each <34>chemotherapy infusion</34>. He reports a good appetite with stable to increasing weight. He is less active than previously although thinks his energy level remains fairly good. He denies any <35>diarrhea</35>. He has some mild chronic <36>L-sided abdominal discomfort</36>. He does have treatment-related. 
