Mr. ***** is a 69 y.o. male whom I am seeing at our GI Oncology practice at the <1>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</1> in follow up for his resected <2>ampullary cancer</2>.     History of Present Illness, reviewed:  This pleasant gentleman's oncologic history is as follows:     March 2021: Developed <3>diarrhea</3> and <4>obstructive jaundice</4> symptoms shortly after undergoing <5>arthroscopic knee surgery</5>   04/20/2021: Presented to local <6>ED</6> and admitted with <7>abdominal pain</7> and <8>jaundice</8>. <9>Total bilirubin</9> <10>6.1</10> on admission.   <11>Abdominal U/S</11> and <12>CT scan</12> (04/20/2021) notable for <13>moderate dilatation of the intra and extrahepatic biliary ducts</13>, <14>dilated CBD</14> tapering at the <15>pancreatic head</15>; no discrete mass appreciated   <16>ERCP</16> (04/20/2021): <17>Abnormal ampulla</17> noted. <18>Pancreatic duct</18> was <19>2.8 mm</19> in the body and <20>4.2 mm</20> in the head. No obvious mass lesion. <21>Bile ducts</21> upstream were dilated, as well as the <22>gallbladder</22>. The <23>common bile duct</23> measured <24>15 mm</24>. A distal narrowing with a <25>1 cm stricture</25> was documented. <26>Thickened duct</26> with upstream dilatation. Underwent <27>sphincterotomy</27>, <28>balloon sweep</28>. <29>Biliary stent</29> placed. <30>Duct brushings pathology</30> noted as <31>atypical</31>, favored to represent <32>reactive/degenerative change</32>. Patient did develop some <33>post-procedural pancreatitis</33>.   05/02/2021: Consult with Dr. ***** ***** (<34>UCSF Surg Onc</34>); index of suspicion high of a <35>ampullary carcinoma</35>, recommended ***** procedure, which was performed on 05/16/2021 at ***** ***** Hospital. Final pathology:  -----------------  <36>Tumor Site</36>:  <37>Intra-ampullary</37>: <38>Ampullary ductal</38>   (<39>pancreaticobiliary-type</39>)   <40>Histologic Type</40>:  <41>Adenocarcinoma</41>, <42>pancreaticobiliary type</42>   <43>Histologic Grade</43>:  <44>G2 Moderately differentiated</44> (50% to 95% of tumor   composed of glands)   <45>Tumor Size</45>:  <46>3.2 cm</46>   <47>Extent Of Tumor</47>:  Invades into <48>duodenal submucosa</48> and <49>muscularis propria</49>, extends more than <50>0.5 cm</50> into <51>pancreas</51> and extends into <52>peripancreatic soft tissue</52>   <53>Margins</53>: All margins negative for invasive 
``` 
