ner_results: ```plaintext
Dr. ***** is a 66 y.o. male who comes to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center in consultation for his <1>metastatic pancreatic cancer</1>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This pleasant gentleman first noted <2>upper abdominal discomfort</2> radiating to his <3>back</3> in early January, which lasted several days to weeks. ***** provided some relief, and he suspected these symptoms may have represented <4>alcoholic pancreatitis</4> as his <5>alcohol consumption</5> had increased around that time. After he stopped drinking completely, the pain eventually subsided. He has had previous episodes of similar pain lasting a couple days and also subsiding after cessation of alcohol use. During this most recent episode, he saw his gastroenterologist Dr. ***** ***** who ordered a <6>CT abdomen</6> (01/26/20) that showed a <7>3.3 x 3.1 x 2.2 cystic mass</7> in the <8>tail</8> with <9>surrounding fat stranding</9>, with <10>extension of the mass beyond the margin of the pancreatic parenchyma</10>, where it abutted the <11>undersurface of the gastric body</11>. This was felt to be possibly c/w a <12>peripancreatic fluid collection/pseudocyst</12> in the setting of <13>pancreatitis</13>, vs a <14>pancreatic cystic neoplasm</14>. <15>F/u MRI abdomen</15> (03/22/20) showed a <16>4.1 x 2.8 cm unilocular cystic lesion</16> in the <17>pancreatic tail</17> with a <18>5 mm mural nodule</18>, at which time <19>surgical consultation</19> was recommended. He was seen by Dr. ***** ***** of our Surgical faculty practice at ***** on March 27, who ordered <20>repeat imaging</20> (<21>CT pancreas protocol</21>). <22>CT C/A/P</22> (04/13/20) showed <23>persistent visualization of the cystic mass within the pancreatic tail</23>, as well as an <24>indeterminate hypodense lesion in the posterior right hepatic lobe</24>. This was followed by a <25>PET-CT</25> (04/28/20) confirming a <26>complex cystic and solid mass in the tail of the pancreas</26> (approximately <27>3 x 2 cm</27>) with <28>SUV 9.1</28>, with features consistent with a <29>primary pancreatic</29>
``` 
