no ***** per above <1>PET</1> - continues on clinical trial, taking intermittent <2>dabrafenib</2> +***** ***** then <3>trametinib</3> alone *****, tolerating well. September 2015 <4>CT C/A/P</4>: Multiple hyperattenuating <5>liver lesions</5>, two of which are new from <6>05/17/2015</6>, are suspicious for <7>metastatic disease</7>. Additional hyperattenuating <8>liver lesions</8> are unchanged from the prior examination dated <9>05/17/2015</9> but are new when compared with <10>PET/CT</10> from <11>01/27/2015</11>. September 2015 <12>Liver FNA</12>: although the cytologic findings are atypical, the population of cells is too small for definitive characterization; re-biopsy could be considered, as clinically indicated. <13>12/28/2015</13> <14>Petct Whole Body Vertex To Toes</14>: 1. No evidence of <15>FDG avid disease</15>. 2. Allowing for differences in technique, no significant interval change of previously described <16>hepatic lesions</16> dating back to <17>2014</17>. December 2015 noted to have <18>diarrhea</18>, and given h/o <19>pNET</19>, added on <20>chromogranin-A</20> and referred to *****. *****. <21>02/02/16</21> - - <22>Black stools</22>: recommend f/u with <23>PMD</23> for work-up, including <24>EGD</24> +/- <25>colonoscopy</25> <26>02/07/16</26> - Patient seen by Dr. ***** ***** <27>02/24/2016</27> <28>Ga-68</28> *****-***** <29>PET/MRI abdomen</29>: :1. Several foci of perceptible receptor uptake within the <30>liver</30>, compatible with ***** lesion seen on <31>MRI</31>. Similar in size and number to the enhancing <32>liver lesions</32> on the <33>CT</33> dated <34>12/28/2015</34> and concerning for <35>metastatic disease</35>. 2. Evidence of receptor positive metastatic <36>osseous lesions</36> within a mid left lateral rib, <37>L1</37> and <38>L3</38>. 3. Findings of receptor positive metastatic disease in the region of the <39>celiac axis</39>, may represent a metastatic <40>lymph node</40> <41>02/24/16</41> <42>LIVER MRI</42> (part of *****-***** <43>PET/MRI</43>): IMPRESSION: 1. Since <44>12/28/2015</44>, unchanged size of multiple peripherally enhancing <45>hepatic metastases</45> which again measure up to <46>2.6 cm</46>. These lesions display <47>somatostatin radiotracer uptake</47> and are likely of <48>pancreatic neuroendocrine origin</48> as opposed to <49>melanoma</49>. 2.
