ner_results: ```plaintext
findings of (relatively subtle) <1>disease progression</1> in <2>lung</2> and <3>liver</3>, she resumed <4>chemotherapy</4> with the same <5>gemcitabine</5>/<6>paclitaxel</6> regimen to which she responded well previously. She again showed good <7>disease control</7> on this regimen, and was taken back for a second <8>liver operation</8> (<9>June 2019</9>) for <10>wedge resection</10> of her isolated <11>hepatic metastasis</11>. She has since opted to resume <12>chemotherapy</12> with <13>gemcitabine</13>/<14>paclitaxel</14>, well-tolerated to date. Postoperative <15>scans</15> have revealed no evidence of active <16>disease</16> in the <17>liver</17> and a slightly growing <18>RLL pulmonary lesion</18> (reviewed at ***** ***** *****; potentially c/w evolving scar tissue), unchanged in appearance on today's <19>scans</19>, as well as <20>sub-cm L-sided lung lesions</20>. Our <21>Thoracic Surgical team</21> has seen the patient and appropriately is not inclined to repeat <22>pulmonary surgery</22> at this juncture, although I think it is reasonable to leave this (vs *****) open as a future possibility. I would suggest continuing with the same regimen and following her with repeat <23>imaging</23> in another 2-3 months' time, unless her <24>tumor marker</24>-9 markedly rises in the interim. Assuming continued good <25>disease control</25>, we will want to continue re-addressing the incorporation of a <26>chemotherapy holiday</26> at some point. We are also keeping in mind future <27>clinical trial possibilities</27> at ***** that may be options for her when she progresses more significantly (subject to change): - A study combining <28>paclitaxel</28> and a <29>glucocorticoid receptor antagonist</29> in the <30>3L-plus setting</30> for <31>metastatic pancreatic cancer</31> (anticipated opening: <32>mid-2020</32>). - Other studies through our <33>Early Phase Developmental Therapeutics</33> and <34>Cancer Immunotherapy Programs</34> that have <35>pancreas-specific therapies</35> or at least are <36>disease agnostic</36> that allow enrollment of <37>pancreatic cancer patients</37>. These include several <38>small molecule inhibitors</38>; a *****-directed <39>mRNA vaccine</39>; and a <40>shared neoantigen adenoviral/mRNA vaccine</40>.
``` 
