 placement.    She received <1>neoadjuvant</1> ***** in the setting of a borderline resectable <2>pancreatic cancer</2> and completed <3>12 cycles</3> with dose modification/delays due to <4>thrombocytopenia</4>.  She underwent ***** <5>procedure</5> on <6>08/18/16</6>. The <7>pathology review</7> of the <8>surgical specimen</8> showed <9>grade 3 treatment response</9> indicating that there was minimal treatment response to the <10>neoadjuvant</10> *****.     Post-operatively, she presented with worsening <11>abdominal distention</11>, increased <12>fatigue</12> and <13>BLE edema</13>. *****, ***** <14>scans</14> were concerning for <15>ascites</15> with suggestion of <16>peritoneal enhancement</16> (no nodules), <17>lymphadenopathy</17> with some of the <18>lymph nodes</18> encasing vessels in <19>celiac trunk</19> and <20>mesenteric root</20>. She was admitted for an expedited work-up and had <21>paracentesis</21>. <22>Cytopathology</22> from the <23>paracentesis</23> revealed <24>3 cells</24> that were morphologically similar to her <25>tumor</25>.     A repeat <26>CT abdomen</26> was obtained on <27>10/17/16</27> and revealed <28>ascites</28> with areas of <29>peritoneal enhancement</29> of the pelvis, however with slightly increased <30>omental stranding</30> in the left upper and lower quadrants as well as increased infiltrative <31>retroperitoneal soft tissue</31> in the <32>portocaval region</32> which were suspicious for <33>peritoneal carcinomatosis</33>. In addition, <34>CA 19-9</34> has increased to <35>1008</35> indicating progression of <36>disease</36>.     She was started on <37>therapy</37> with <38>gemcitabine</38> + <39>capecitabine</39> on <40>10/20/16</40>. <41>Cycle 1, day 8</41> was delayed due to <42>cytopenias</42>. She was admitted on <43>cycle 2, day 3</43> for <44>constipation</44>. She has continued to decline clinically during this time period with decline in her <45>PS</45>, <46>weight</46> and worsening <47>ascites</47>.     During today's visit, I had a long conversation with her and her husband regarding <48>goals of care</48>. I shared with them my concern regarding her <49>clinical decline</49> and the most recent <50>symptoms</50> related to <51>disease progression</51>. I informed them that she is not strong enough to tolerate the <52>gemcitabine</52> combined with <53>capecitabine</53> and that we may even consider 
``` 
